# Nothing to build — this is a zero-dependency static app.
# These are here so an exported project or a stray export never lands in the repo.
*.log
*.tmp
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# local scratch
scratch/
*.bak
*~

# Scratch drawings dropped in the test folder are not worth keeping. Test pages
# must not read a sibling file, though: this rule sat here silently excluding
# test/fixture.dxf, so the underlay suite passed on every local run and failed on
# CI because the file had never been committed. The reference DXF is inlined in
# the page that needs it.
test/*.dxf

# tools/*.json used to be ignored, with a note saying client data must stay out of
# the repository. That was the right default and it is now a deliberate
# exception: the WXM project is committed, because this repository is private and
# the site this deploys to is dedicated to that one project.
#
# Two things to know before adding a THIRD project file here. The rule that
# mattered was never "not in git forever" — it was "one client's installation data
# must not be visible to another". A private repository satisfies that for as
# long as it stays private, and it stops the day anyone else is given access.
# If that ever happens this line goes back and the VM carries the file the way a
# deploy carries a secret.
#
# And history is not the present. Removing a file from HEAD does not remove it
# from any commit that already holds it, so this decision is not reversible by
# re-adding the rule. It is reversible by new repository.

# The earlier iteration of the same project, kept out of the repository. It is
# the same client's data and a superseded draft — 97 circuits against 97, nothing
# in it that the WXM file above does not have. Committing two copies of one
# client's installation is how the superseded one gets quoted by mistake, so only
# the current file is committed.
tools/roeslein-line5.json

